Actual_output:
Here's an example of a validation error message that confirms an invalid email address format:

**Error Message**
Invalid Email Format

**Example Code (HTML)**
```
<p>Invalid email format</p>
<p>Examples: <b>username</b>.<b>domain</b></p>
```

**Example Code (CSS)**
```
#error-format {
  color: red;
}
```

This error message clearly conveys that the provided email address is invalid and highlights an example of a correct format. It also provides a visual cue by highlighting the text in red, making it more attention-grabbing.

Alternatively, you can use HTML and CSS to create a custom validation error message with a specific design:

```
<style>
  #error-format {
    color: red;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: bold;
    padding: 5px 10px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    text-align: center;
  }
</style>

<p id="error-format">Invalid Email Format</p>
```

This code creates a custom error message with a specific design, making it more visually appealing and effective at conveying the expected outcome.
